-- title:
--   Directed supersingular-fibre dictionary for the Γ₁(ℓ_g)-rigid moduli data
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime, $M'$ a non-zero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and $\ell_g$ a prime with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ that divides $M'$ (the membership $\ell_g \in M'.\mathrm{primeFactors}$ being recorded separately, as it is used as an index).
--
--   **Reduction data.** $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` with `A.LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $q$ is a non-unit of $A$; $W$ is a finite set of places of the field `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` over the residue field of $A$, and `hW` says that $W$ consists exactly of the supersingular places `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)`; `hle` says that the base-changed full modular function field `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` (the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-subfield of $\overline{\mathbb Q}((t))$ generated by the coefficientwise image of `modularFunctionFieldFull M'`) is contained in `fieldBar q M'`, the level-`levelH q M'` function field of level $q^2M'$; $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` along $A$ with values in `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'`, so it consists of a valuation subring `R₀.integers`, a surjective residue homomorphism onto the target field whose kernel is the maximal ideal, a map on places preserving degrees, and the compatibilities with $A$ and with divisors recorded in that structure; `hR₀` requires that for every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise image in $\overline{\mathbb Q}((t))$ lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, that image lies in `R₀.integers` and its $R_0$-residue is, as a Laurent series over the residue field of $A$, the coefficientwise reduction of $y$. Finally $s$ is a chosen element of $W$, i.e. a supersingular place.
--
--   **Small-constant data.** $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$ and $\pi_0 \in k_0$ an element lying in $A$. Writing $A_0 =$ `A.comap (algebraMap k₀ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))` for the induced valuation ring of $k_0$, the hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres` say that $A_0$ is a discrete valuation ring, that its maximal ideal is generated by $\pi_0$, that it is henselian local, and that its residue field $\kappa =$ `ResidueField A₀` is algebraically closed; `hκ` says that every element of $A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to an element of $k_0 \cap A$.
--
--   **Level and root-of-unity data.** $\xi \in k_0$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity, and `hιξ` provides a ring homomorphism $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb C$ with $\iota(\xi) = e^{2\pi i/q}$. $H_1$ is the subgroup of $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ given by `hH₁` as the intersection of `levelH q M'` (the kernel of reduction to $(\mathbb Z/q)^\times$) with the kernel of reduction to $(\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times$. $K$ is the intermediate field of $k_0((t))/k_0$ equal, by `hK`, to the base change `laurentBaseChange k₀ (xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`; algebra and scalar-tower instances relate $A_0$, $k_0$ and $K$. The element $j \in K$ has Laurent expansion `coeffEmb k₀ jq` and is non-zero. The relevant chart is $C =$ [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel.chartAlgFin A₀ K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L142), the $A_0$-subalgebra of $K$ attached to $j$, and the index set of the conclusion is the set of maximal ideals $y$ of $C$ containing the image of $\pi_0$, i.e. the closed points of the special fibre of that chart.
--
--   **Moduli functoriality data.** `hℓ` requires that for every $A_0$-algebra $T$, `IsGamma1Point W ℓg D` is preserved by variable changes, $D$ being transported by `LevelPData.variableChange`; `hM` requires likewise that `IsGamma0PowAt W p k h` is preserved, $h$ being transported by `kernelVariableChangeDeg C (gamma0PowDeg p k)`. Further, $\mathcal G$ is a family of relative group laws `GroupLaws A₀` which is chord-tangent (`h𝒢`) and has the origin as identity (`h𝒢O`), and $\mathcal T$ is a `LevelTransport A₀ 𝒢 q` which is a section transport (`h𝒯`), i.e. its variable-change and base-change actions on raw Drinfeld pairs are compatible with the corresponding maps of projective models. The hypotheses `hVC` and `hCO` assert that for every $A_0$-algebra $T$ (resp. every $A_0$-algebra map $f : T \to T'$) and every projective Weierstrass curve over $T$ there is a graded ring homomorphism of the graded rings `projModelGradingCR`, with the stated inequality for the irrelevant ideals, realising the variable change (`IsVariableChangeHom`), resp. the coefficient map (`IsCoefficientHom`).
--
--   Set $\mathcal R =$ `rigidDataGamma1Pow A₀ ℓg M' q hℓ hM 𝒢 𝒯`, the rigid Weierstrass moduli datum obtained from the product of the $\Gamma_0$-power component at $M'$, the $\Gamma_1$ component at $\ell_g$ and the Drinfeld level-$q$ component. A raw point $x \in \mathcal R.\mathrm{Raw}\,\kappa$ consists of a Weierstrass curve `x.curve` over $\kappa$ with unit discriminant, together with level data `x.level`: a family `x.level.1` of kernel polynomials indexed by the prime factors of $M'$, a `LevelPData` `x.level.2.1` with coordinates $x_P,y_P,x_Q,y_Q$, and a raw Drinfeld pair `x.level.2.2`, all satisfying the level conditions. Write $\mathcal R.\mathrm{Pt}\,\kappa$ for the quotient of $\mathcal R.\mathrm{Raw}\,\kappa$ by the variable-change relation, and $\mathcal R.\mathrm{act}$ for the action of variable changes on raw points.
--
--   Say that a closed special point $y$ of $C$ **lies over $s$** when the following holds: for every Laurent series $g$ over $\mathbb Q$ belonging to `modularFunctionFieldFull M'` whose image in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` lies in `R₀.integers`, such that $g$ is regular at every place of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ at which $j_q$ is regular (in terms of the order function `Place.ord`), and such that the $R_0$-residue of $g$ lies in the valuation subring of $s$, and for every proof that `coeffEmb k₀ g` lies in $K$ and in the chart $C$, one has: for every $c \in k_0$ with $c \in A$ whose residue in `ResidueField A` equals the value `s.evalAt` of the $R_0$-residue of $g$, the difference between $g$ (viewed in $C$) and the image of $c$ lies in $y$.
--
--   The assertion is the existence of a map
--   $$\Phi : \{\,y \subseteq C \text{ maximal},\ \pi_0 \in y\,\} \longrightarrow \mathcal R.\mathrm{Raw}\,\kappa$$
--   with the following five properties.
--
--   (i) For every $y$ there are $x_G, y_G \in \kappa$ such that the affine Weierstrass equation of $(\Phi y).\mathrm{curve}$ holds at $(x_G,y_G)$, the polynomial $((\Phi y).\mathrm{level}.1)$ at the index $\ell_g$ evaluates to $0$ at $x_G$, and, on the base change of $(\Phi y).\mathrm{curve}$ to $\kappa$, the point `LevelRelabelling.toPoint` attached to the $\Gamma_1$-coordinates $x_P,y_P$ of $(\Phi y).\mathrm{level}.2.1$ equals $\ell_g^{\,v_{\ell_g}(M')-1}$ times the point attached to $(x_G,y_G)$, where $v_{\ell_g}(M') =$ `M'.factorization ℓg`.
--
--   (ii) For every $y$ lying over $s$, and every point $P$ of the affine curve $(\Phi y).\mathrm{curve}$ over $\kappa$, the relation $q \cdot P = 0$ forces $P = 0$; that is, the curve attached to such a $y$ has no non-trivial $q$-torsion over $\kappa$.
--
--   (iii) For all $y, y'$ both lying over $s$ there is a variable change $C_0$ over $\kappa$ such that $\mathcal R.\mathrm{act}\,C_0\,(\Phi y)$ has the same curve as $\Phi y'$, the same $\Gamma_0$-component `level.1` as $\Phi y'$, and such that [`ModularCurve.InLine`](def/ModularCurve_KatzLevelP.html#L21) holds for $(\Phi y').\mathrm{curve}$ at $\ell_g$ for the pair consisting of the $x_P$ of $\Phi y'$ and the $x_P$ of $\mathcal R.\mathrm{act}\,C_0\,(\Phi y)$: there is $a$ with $1 \le a \le (\ell_g-1)/2$ and $x \cdot (\Psi_a^2)(x_0) = \Phi_a(x_0)$ for the two $x$-coordinates in question.
--
--   (iv) (Directed relabelling clause.) Let $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ lie in $\Gamma(q)$ and in $\Gamma_0(M')$, and let $\tau$ be a $k_0$-algebra automorphism of $K$ which is a level automorphism at $\gamma$ in the sense of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt k₀ q ξ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29): for all weights $k$ and all modular forms $f,g$ for $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$, $p_g$ having non-zero rational series, and for every $x \in K$ whose Laurent expansion is the image of the quotient of these series, and every ring homomorphism $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb C$ sending $\xi$ to $e^{2\pi i/q}$, the series $\iota(\tau x)$ times the $q$-expansion of $g \mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma$ equals the $q$-expansion of $f \mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma$. Assume further that $\tau$ maps the chart $C$ into itself. Then for all closed special points $y, y_1$ such that $y_1$ is the $\tau$-image of $y$, in the sense that for every $b \in C$ with $\tau b \in C$ one has $b \in y$ if and only if $\tau b \in y_1$, and for all raw points $x, x_1 \in \mathcal R.\mathrm{Raw}\,\kappa$ with the discriminant of the Drinfeld component $x.\mathrm{level}.2.2.\mathrm{curve}$ a unit, the following implication holds: if $x$ and $\Phi y$ define the same class in $\mathcal R.\mathrm{Pt}\,\kappa$, and $x_1.\mathrm{curve} = x.\mathrm{curve}$, and $x_1.\mathrm{level}.1 = x.\mathrm{level}.1$, and the point of the base-changed curve attached to the $\Gamma_1$-coordinates of $x_1$ equals $\gamma_{00}$ times the point attached to those of $x$ (the scalar being the $(0,0)$ entry of $\gamma$), and $x_1$ has $x_Q = x_P$, $y_Q = y_P$, and $x_1.\mathrm{level}.2.2$ is the relabelling `RawDrinfeldPair.relabel 𝒢 γ` of $x.\mathrm{level}.2.2$, then $\Phi y_1$ and $x_1$ define the same class in $\mathcal R.\mathrm{Pt}\,\kappa$.
--
--   (v) For all closed special points $y, y'$ such that $y'$ lies over $s$: if $\Phi y$ and $\Phi y'$ define the same class in $\mathcal R.\mathrm{Pt}\,\kappa$, then $y = y'$. (The hypothesis of lying over $s$ is imposed here on $y'$ only.)
--
--   This is the guarded supersingular-fibre dictionary in its directed form: it attaches to each closed point of the special fibre of the integral chart of $X_{H_1}(q^2M')$ a Weierstrass curve over the algebraically closed residue field together with $\Gamma_0(M')$-, $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$- and Drinfeld level-$q$ structure, with supersingularity and alignment over the chosen supersingular place $s$, injectivity on moduli classes, and — the directed clause — the matching of a level automorphism $\tau$ attached to $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ with relabelling of the level structures by $\gamma$, the $\Gamma_1$-point being multiplied by the entry $\gamma_{00}$. It is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.exists_ssFibreDictionary_chartAlgFin_rigidDataGamma1Pow`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.exists_ssFibreDictionary_chartAlgFin_rigidDataGamma1Pow) and by the two comparisons of the orders of level-automorphism stabilisers with the orders of automorphism groups at moduli places in the residue characteristics $3$ and $2$.
